var definitions = ['<h2>Très peu de gens lisent des livres tous les jours.</h2><div><strong>Très</strong> This is the French adverb for "very". It is used here to intensify the degree of the phrase "peu de gens" which follows.</div><div><strong>peu</strong> This is a French word meaning "little" or "few". Combined with "très" it emphasizes the small number of people referred to.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This French preposition is used in various ways, but here it is part of the expression "peu de" which means "few" or "a small number of".</div><div><strong>gens</strong> This is a French word for "people" or "folks". It is a plural noun referring to people in a general sense.</div><div><strong>lisent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "lire", which means "to read". It describes the action being performed by the subject of the sentence, "gens".</div><div><strong>des</strong> This French article is the plural of "un" or "une", and can mean "some" or "any". Here, it introduces the plural noun "livres".</div><div><strong>livres</strong> This is a French word for "books". It is a plural noun and is the direct object of the verb "lisent", indicating what the people are reading.</div><div><strong>tous les jours</strong> This French phrase means "every day". It is an expression of frequency, explaining how often the action of reading books takes place.</div>','<h2>J’ai très peu de photos de mon voyage.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French "je" combined with the following verb, using an apostrophe due to the following vowel sound, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It indicates possession or status of existence in this context.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very". It’s used here to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>peu</strong> This is a noun or adverb which means "little" or "few". In this sentence, it functions as a noun phrase in combination with "de" to express a small quantity.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition which, in this context, is translated as "of" in English. It is used to link the noun phrase "très peu" with "photos".</div><div><strong>photos</strong> This is a plural noun meaning "photos" or "pictures". It is the object of the preposition "de" indicating what the speaker has a small quantity of.</div><div><strong>de mon</strong> The preposition "de" combined with the possessive adjective "mon" translates to "of my" in English. </div><div><strong>voyage</strong> This is a noun meaning "trip" or "journey". Here, it indicates that the photos are from the speaker’s travels.</div>','<h2>Elle s’intéresse à très peu de films.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>s’intéresse</strong> This is a reflexive form of the verb "intéresser", which means "to be interested in" in English. The "s’" before the verb indicates that it is reflexive, meaning the action refers back to the subject. In this case, "s’intéresse" is conjugated in the third-person singular present tense.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a French preposition that means "to" in English. It is used here to indicate the direction of interest towards something.</div><div><strong>très peu de</strong> This phrase translates to "very few" in English. "Très" means "very", and "peu" means "few" or "little", so together they indicate a small quantity or a limited extent.</div><div><strong>films</strong> This is the plural form of "film" in French, which translates to "films" or "movies" in English. It is the object of interest in the sentence.</div>','<h2>Nous avons quelques minutes avant notre pause déjeuner.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it indicates possession of time before the lunch break.</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This is an indefinite adjective meaning "a few" or "some" in English.</div><div><strong>minutes</strong> This is a noun meaning "minutes" in English. It’s used here in the plural form to indicate a span of time.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a preposition meaning "before" in English. It’s used to indicate temporal succession.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "our" in English. It denotes ownership or association with the noun that follows, which in this sentence is "pause déjeuner".</div><div><strong>pause</strong> This is a noun meaning "break" in English. When combined with "déjeuner", it refers specifically to the lunch break.</div><div><strong>déjeuner</strong> This is a noun meaning "lunch" in English. When combined with "pause", it forms a compound noun, "pause déjeuner", which means "lunch break".</div>','<h2>Il ne me reste que quelques dollars.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" or "it" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence and often used with impersonal expressions.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is part of the negation in French. It’s used in combination with another negative word to negate a verb. In written French, "ne" precedes the verb, though it’s often dropped in spoken French.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is a first-person singular reflexive or indirect object pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" or "myself" in English. It indicates that the action of the verb is directed towards the speaker.</div><div><strong>reste</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "rester", which means "to remain" or "to be left". It conveys what remains or is left of something.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This word has multiple uses in French; in this context, it is part of the negative expression "ne...que", which translates to "only" or "nothing but" in English, indicating limitation.</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This is an adjective meaning "some" or "a few". It is used here to indicate a small, indefinite quantity.</div><div><strong>dollars</strong> This is a noun, the plural form of "dollar". It’s a unit of currency, and though not a uniquely French word, it is used in France to refer to the US currency.</div>','<h2>J’ai quelques questions à vous poser.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of "je", the first-person singular pronoun in French (equivalent to "I" in English), and occurs before vowels or mute h’s. It forms part of the complex tense "j’ai" (I have).</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It’s used here to construct the phrase "j’ai" (I have) as part of the sentence.</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This means "some" or "a few" in English. It is used to indicate an indefinite small number of something, in this case, "questions".</div><div><strong>questions</strong> This is the plural form of "question", which means the same in English. It’s the direct object of the sentence; these are the things being had, hence "I have some questions".</div><div><strong>à</strong> This means "to" in English when indicating direction or purpose. In this sentence, it’s part of the construction "à vous poser" which means "to ask you".</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It can denote a formal address or refer to more than one person. Here, it’s the indirect object of the verb "poser", indicating to whom the questions are to be asked.</div><div><strong>poser</strong> This verb means "to pose" or "to ask" in English, and it’s in its infinitive form here. The phrase "à poser" in combination with "vous" translates as "to ask you".</div>','<h2>Il avait d’autres plans.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s used as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It indicates possession or a condition in the past.</div><div><strong>d’autres</strong> This is a contraction of "de autres", which translates to "of other" or "other" in English when referring to plural objects or plans in this case. The apostrophe replaces the "e" in "de" before a vowel sound to ease pronunciation.</div><div><strong>plans</strong> This is the French word for "plans". Similar to English, it refers to intentions or arranged activities for the future.</div>','<h2>Nous n’avons pas d’autre option.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>n’avons</strong> This is the contraction of "ne" (negation) and "avons", which is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", meaning "to have". The "ne...pas" construction negates the verb, expressing the absence of something or stating that something is not done. In everyday spoken French, the "ne" is often dropped, leaving just "pas".</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation ("ne...pas"), which surrounds the verb to negate it. In French, you typically need both "ne" and "pas" to form a negation, though as mentioned above, "ne" is often omitted in spoken language.</div><div><strong>d’autre</strong> This is a contraction of "de" (of/from) and "autre" (other/additional). When combined, it means "of another" or "other".</div><div><strong>option</strong> This is the French word for "option" or "choice". It’s a feminine singular noun in French.</div>','<h2>Il n’y a pas d’autres vols vers Paris aujourd’hui.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "it" or "there" in English when used with "y a", forming an impersonal expression.</div><div><strong>n’y a pas</strong> This is the negative form of "il y a", which means "there is" or "there are" in English. "n’" is the contraction of "ne", indicating negation, and "pas" completes the negative expression, negating the existence of something.</div><div><strong>d’autres</strong> The combination of "de" (of) and "autres" (other) translates to "other" or "any other" in English. It suggests additional entities or alternatives.</div><div><strong>vols</strong> This is the plural form of "vol", which means "flight" in English. It’s the main noun of the sentence, referring to multiple airplane trips.</div><div><strong>vers</strong> This is a preposition meaning "towards" in English. It indicates direction or destination.</div><div><strong>Paris</strong> This is a proper noun, the capital city of France. In the sentence, it is the destination of the flights.</div><div><strong>aujourd’hui</strong> This is an adverb meaning "today" in English. It indicates when the action of the verb is taking place.</div>','<h2>Elle peut lire n’importe quoi dans la bibliothèque.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>peut</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It indicates the ability of the subject to perform the action.</div><div><strong>lire</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to read" in French. Placed after "peut", it shows the action the subject is able to perform.</div><div><strong>n’importe quoi</strong> This phrase translates to "anything" in English. "N’importe" literally means "no matter", and "quoi" is "what", so, in combination, it implies indifference to the choice.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French meaning "in" or "inside". It is used here to indicate location.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article for feminine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>bibliothèque</strong> This is a French word for "library". It’s a feminine singular noun referring to a place that houses a collection of books for reading or borrowing.</div>','<h2>Ils feront n’importe quoi pour passer l’examen.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of people.</div><div><strong>feront</strong> This is the third-person plural future tense of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". In this sentence, it indicates what the group of people will do in the future.</div><div><strong>n’importe quoi</strong> This is a French phrase meaning "anything". It is made up of "ne...importe" which is a negation, and "quoi" which means "what". Together they imply a sense of "whatever", indicating that the group will take any action necessary.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition in French translated as "for" in English. It indicates the purpose or reason behind the action.</div><div><strong>passer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to pass" or "to get through". In this context, it refers to successfully completing something, like an exam.</div><div><strong>l’examen</strong> This is a French masculine singular noun meaning "the exam" or "the test". The article "l’" is a contraction of "le", which is required in front of a vowel or mute h, and "examen" is the noun being referred to.</div>','<h2>Il ferait n’importe quoi pour revenir en arrière.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ferait</strong> This is the third-person singular conditional tense form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". In this context, it translates to "would do".</div><div><strong>n’importe quoi</strong> This is a French expression meaning "anything" or "whatever". It emphasizes the extent to which someone is willing to go; here, it implies doing anything or whatever it takes.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "for" in English. It is used to indicate the purpose or reason for an action.</div><div><strong>revenir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "revenir", which means "to come back" or "to return". In this sentence, it refers to the action of going back in time or to a previous situation.</div><div><strong>en arrière</strong> This is a French phrase that translates to "backwards" or "in reverse" in English. In the context of the sentence, it suggests a desire to go back to the past or to undo something that has happened.</div>','<h2>En connaissant le français, tu peux voyager n’importe où en France.</h2><div><strong>En</strong> In this context, "En" is a preposition used in French to express being in the situation of or while doing something. It often precedes a gerund (a verb form ending in -ant that functions as a noun).</div><div><strong>connaissant</strong> This is the present participle of the French verb "connaître", which means "to know" or "to be familiar with". Here it indicates the action of knowing or being familiar.</div><div><strong>le français</strong> This phrase translates as "the French [language]" in English.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking to one person informally.</div><div><strong>peux</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It expresses capability or possibility.</div><div><strong>voyager</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French meaning "to travel".</div><div><strong>n’importe où</strong> Literally translating to "no matter where", this phrase means "anywhere" in English.</div><div><strong>en France</strong> This phrase means "in France" in English and designates the location where the action of traveling can take place.</div>','<h2>Je voudrais aller n’importe où dans le monde.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>voudrais</strong> This is the first-person singular conditional tense of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It expresses a polite desire or wish.</div><div><strong>aller</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to go". In this sentence, it’s used after "voudrais" to express what the speaker would like to do.</div><div><strong>n’importe où</strong> This phrase translates to "anywhere" in English. "N’importe" means "any", and "où" means "where", together expressing an indifference to the destination.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition that means "in" in English. It introduces the context or location.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article "the" in English, used here for the masculine singular noun that follows.</div><div><strong>monde</strong> This is a masculine singular noun meaning "world". It is the object of the preposition "dans", completing the phrase by specifying the place where the subject wishes to go.</div>','<h2>C’est un bon livre pour n’importe qui.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (this) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense form of "être", which means "to be". Together, "c’est" translates to "it is" or "this is" in English.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite masculine singular article, which translates to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>bon</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "good". It is placed before the noun and agrees with the masculine singular form of the noun it describes.</div><div><strong>livre</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French, which means "book".</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition that translates to "for" in English. It’s used to indicate the beneficiary or recipient of an action.</div><div><strong>n’importe qui</strong> This is a phrase meaning "anyone" or "whoever". It’s a combination of "ne" (negation), "importe" (from the verb "importer" meaning to matter), and "qui" (who). "N’importe qui" can be used to refer to any person without specifying who.</div>','<h2>N’importe qui peut chanter cette chanson drôle.</h2><div><strong>N’importe qui</strong> This is a French expression meaning "anyone". It suggests that the action or characteristic described can be done by any person without any specific requirements or skills.</div><div><strong>peut</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It indicates the ability or capacity to do something.</div><div><strong>chanter</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to sing" in French. It is used here to describe the action that can be performed by anyone.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French that means "this". It is used to specify the noun that follows and agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which in this case is feminine and singular.</div><div><strong>chanson</strong> This is a French noun meaning "song". It is the object of the verb "chanter" and is feminine and singular to match the demonstrative adjective "cette".</div><div><strong>drôle</strong> This is an adjective in French meaning "funny" or "humorous". It describes the noun "chanson", and agrees with it in gender and number, being feminine and singular.</div>','<h2>N’importe qui peut apprendre une nouvelle langue.</h2><div><strong>N’importe qui</strong> This is a French phrase meaning "anyone". It indicates that any person without exception has the ability referred to.</div><div><strong>peut</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "can" or "to be able to". It indicates possibility or capability.</div><div><strong>apprendre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the French verb meaning "to learn". In this sentence, it is the main action that "n’importe qui" is able to do.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, translating to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>nouvelle</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "new". In this context, it modifies the noun "langue". It follows the gender and number of the noun it describes and here is feminine singular to match "langue".</div><div><strong>langue</strong> This is a French noun meaning "language". It is feminine singular in this sentence.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>Ils sont un peu trop grands.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the French 3rd person plural pronoun, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s used here as the subject of the sentence and can refer to a group of males, or a mixed-gender group.</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being or condition of the subject.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase translates to "a little" or "somewhat" in English. It is used here to modify the extent or degree of the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>trop</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "too" or "excessively" in English. It is used to indicate that something is more than necessary or desirable.</div><div><strong>grands</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "big" or "tall". Here, it’s used in the masculine plural form to agree with "ils". It refers to the size or height of the subjects. If the subjects were female, it would be "grandes".</div>','<h2>C’est pas un peu ennuyeux ça?</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This contraction stands for "ce est", which translates to "it is" in English. The expression is often used in French to describe a situation or comment on something.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is a French negation word that, when used with "ne" (omitted in informal speech), negates a statement. In this sentence, the "ne" is dropped in spoken or informal language, so "pas" indicates the negative by itself.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the masculine singular indefinite article in French, meaning "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>peu</strong> It means "little" or "a bit" in English. It is often used to indicate a small amount or degree.</div><div><strong>ennuyeux</strong> This is an adjective that means "boring" or "tedious" in English. It agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes, which, in this case, is implied.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun translating to "that" in English. It is used here to refer back to something previously mentioned or understood in the context.</div>','<h2>Tu devrais te reposer un peu.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>devrais</strong> This word is the second-person singular conditional tense of the verb "devoir", which means "must" or "should". In this case, it suggests a recommendation or advice, equivalent to "should" in English.</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun corresponding to "tu", and it is used with reflexive verbs to indicate that the action is performed by the subject on themselves. In English, this concept is translated as "yourself".</div><div><strong>reposer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "reposer", which means "to rest". When combined with a reflexive pronoun, like "te", it means "to rest oneself" or simply "to rest".</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase means "a little" or "a bit" in English. It is used to suggest a small amount or degree of something, in this case, rest.</div>','<h2>Elle se couchait après avoir lu un peu.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se couchait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the reflexive verb "se coucher", which means "to go to bed" or "to lie down". The imperfect tense indicates a past action that was ongoing or habitual.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "after". It is used to indicate that one action follows another in time.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This is the auxiliary verb "have" in English, used here in the infinitive form to create the past infinitive "after having".</div><div><strong>lu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "lire", which means "to read". Combined with "avoir" it creates the past infinitive phrase that indicates the action of reading was completed in the past before going to bed.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase translates to "a little" in English. It quantifies the amount of reading done.</div>','<h2>Tu dois te concentrer un peu plus, c’est tout.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>dois</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "must" or "to have to". Here, it indicates an obligation or a need for the subject to act.</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun in French. It’s used with reflexive verbs and it means that the subject (tu) is also the object of the action (to concentrate oneself).</div><div><strong>concentrer</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French, which means "to concentrate". It’s used here after the conjugated modal verb "devoir" (dois).</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase translates to "a little" in English. It’s adverbial, modifying the verb "concentrer", indicating the extent to which the subject needs to concentrate.</div><div><strong>plus</strong> This word means "more" in English. Put after "un peu", it strengthens the adverbial phrase, equivalent to saying "a little more".</div><div><strong>c’est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense of the verb "être" (to be), which translates to "it is". Here, it’s used with the impersonal subject "ce", referring to a situation or fact.</div><div><strong>tout</strong> This word means "all" or "everything" in English. In this context, it minimizes the action that needs to be taken – just a bit more concentration is all that’s needed.</div>','<h2>Ils recommandent que nous attendions un peu plus longtemps.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. They are the subject of the sentence, indicating the group making the recommendation.</div><div><strong>recommandent</strong> This comes from the verb "recommander", which means "to recommend". It is in the third-person plural present tense, aligning with the subject "Ils".</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction that introduces a subordinate clause. In this context, it means "that", setting up the action that "they" are recommending.</div><div><strong>nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It is the subject of the subordinate clause, indicating who should wait.</div><div><strong>attendions</strong> This is the first-person plural present subjunctive form of the verb "attendre", which means "to wait". The subjunctive mood is used after "que" to express a wish, recommendation, or demand.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This translates to "a little" or "a bit" in English. It qualifies how much longer "we" should wait.</div><div><strong>plus longtemps</strong> This phrase means "longer" in English. Combined with "un peu", it specifies the recommended waiting duration.</div>','<h2>Voyons voir... J’ai fait ma lessive et un peu d’exercice.</h2><div><strong>Voyons</strong> This is a form of the French verb "voir", which means "to see" in English. In this context, "Voyons" is used as an interjection similar to "let’s see" as an invitation to consider something or to think about what’s next.</div><div><strong>voir...</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to see" in English, indicating a suggestion or a thought that is being considered, often trailing off as if leading to the next part of the sentence.</div><div><strong>J’ai fait</strong> This is composed of two parts: "j’ai" (I have) which is the first-person singular present perfect conjugation of the verb "avoir", meaning "to have", and "fait" which is the past participle of "faire", meaning "to do" or "to make". Together, "j’ai fait" means "I have done" or "I did".</div><div><strong>ma lessive</strong> "Ma" is the feminine singular possessive adjective meaning "my" in English, and "lessive" is a French noun meaning "laundry". Therefore, "ma lessive" translates to "my laundry".</div><div><strong>et</strong> The word "et" is a conjunction in French that means "and" in English. It connects words, phrases, or clauses together.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> "Un peu" translates into English as "a little". It is a phrase that can modify nouns to indicate a small quantity or degree.</div><div><strong>d’exercice</strong> This phrase consists of the preposition "de" which translates to "of" in English, and "exercice", a French noun meaning "exercise". Together, "d’exercice" means "exercise" (with "de" indicating an unspecified amount of exercise). In this context, "un peu d’exercice" means "a little bit of exercise".</div>','<h2>Je parle un peu français.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>parle</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "parler", which means "to speak". It describes the action being carried out by the subject.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase means "a little" or "somewhat" in English. It describes the extent or degree of the action.</div><div><strong>français</strong> This is a French noun meaning "French". It refers to the French language in this context. It’s used here as a direct object of the verb "parle", indicating which language is being spoken.</div>','<h2>Désolée, je suis un peu en retard.</h2><div><strong>Désolée</strong> This is the French word for "sorry". It indicates an apology. The word is in the feminine form, indicating that the speaker is female. If the speaker were male it would be "Désolé".</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state or action of the subject.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase translates to "a little" or "a bit" in English. It’s used here to express a small amount or degree of something.</div><div><strong>en retard</strong> This phrase means "late" in English. It indicates that the speaker has arrived after the expected or scheduled time.</div>','<h2>Ouvrez un peu plus grand, s’il vous plaît.</h2><div><strong>Ouvrez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural imperative form of the verb "ouvrir", which means "to open". It is used to give a command or request directed at multiple people or at one person in a formal setting.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase translates to "a little" in English. It is an adverbial phrase that modifies the verb, suggesting a small increase in the action’s intensity.</div><div><strong>plus grand</strong> This phrase means "wider" or "bigger". It’s made up of "plus" (more) and "grand" (big, large, wide). Here, it further modifies the action of opening, asking the listener to open something wider or larger than they currently are.</div><div><strong>s’il vous plaît</strong> This is a polite phrase that translates to "please" in English. It’s made up of "s’il" (if it), "vous" (you), and "plaît" (pleases), literally meaning "if it pleases you". It’s commonly used at the end of a request to be polite.</div>','<h2>Je vais un peu mieux aujourd’hui.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it’s part of the expression "je vais mieux" meaning "I am doing better".</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase translates to "a little" or "somewhat" in English. It is used here to modify the extent of feeling better.</div><div><strong>mieux</strong> This is the comparative form of "bien" (well) in French, meaning "better". It’s an adverb modifying the verb "vais", indicating an improvement in well-being or health.</div><div><strong>aujourd’hui</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "today". It indicates when the improvement in well-being is occurring.</div>','<h2>Oui, ma température est un peu élevée.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is used to affirmatively respond to a question or statement.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective "my" in English, used to indicate ownership or association with the speaker, who is female in this context. It is the feminine singular form, agreeing with the feminine noun "température".</div><div><strong>température</strong> This is a feminine noun in French, translating to "temperature" in English. It refers to the measure of heat or coldness, commonly associated with body temperature or weather.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It links the subject "ma température" with the adjective that describes it.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase means "a little" or "somewhat" in English, indicating a slight amount or degree.</div><div><strong>élevée</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "élever", which means "to raise" or "to elevate" in English. When used as an adjective, as it is here, it means "elevated" or "high". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "température".</div>','<h2>D’accord. C’est un peu tôt, mais ça me va.</h2><div><strong>D’accord</strong> This is the French expression for "okay" or "alright". It indicates agreement or acceptance.</div><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" (it/this) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It’s like saying "It’s" or "This is" in English.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase means "a little" or "somewhat" in English. It is used to indicate a small degree of something.</div><div><strong>tôt</strong> This is the French word for "early". It is an adjective describing the time of an event.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a phrase or clause contrasting with what has been mentioned previously.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This translates to "that" in English. It’s a demonstrative pronoun referring to something previously mentioned or understood in the context.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, in the indirect object form. It’s equivalent to "to me" or "for me" in English.</div><div><strong>va</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". Here, used in the expression "ça me va", it effectively means "that suits me" or "that works for me" in English.</div>','<h2>Tu es un peu pâle, est-ce que tu te sens bien?</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being of the subject.</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase translates to "a little" or "somewhat" in English. It modifies the adjective that follows to indicate a lesser degree.</div><div><strong>pâle</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "pale". It describes someone’s appearance, suggesting a lack of color in the face, often due to illness or fatigue.</div><div><strong>est-ce que</strong> This is a French phrase used to introduce a question. It literally translates to "is it that" but doesn’t have a direct English equivalent as a question phrase.</div><div><strong>tu te sens</strong> This is a reflexive form in the present tense. "Tu" is the subject pronoun "you", and "te sens" is from the reflexive verb "se sentir" which means "to feel". Here it means "you feel".</div><div><strong>bien?</strong> This is an adverb meaning "well" in English. It is often used to inquire about someone’s health or well-being.</div>','<h2>Il insiste pour que nous enquêtions un peu plus.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the French third-person singular pronoun, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>insiste</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "insister", which means "to insist". The subject here is insisting on an action.</div><div><strong>pour que</strong> This phrase is a conjunction used to introduce a subjunctive clause. In English, it roughly translates to "so that" or "in order that".</div><div><strong>nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the subjunctive clause.</div><div><strong>enquêtions</strong> This is the first-person plural subjunctive present tense form of the verb "enquêter", which means "to investigate". It’s used here because the action is something that the speaker wants to happen, rather than something that is definitely happening.</div><div><strong>un peu plus</strong> This phrase translates to "a little more" in English. It suggests that some investigation has already occurred, and the speaker is insisting on doing it to a greater extent.</div>','<h2>Ils manquent quelques coéquipiers.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>manquent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "manquer", which can mean "to miss" or "to lack". In this context, it means "are missing" or "lack".</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "some" or "a few". It modifies the noun that follows, indicating a small number.</div><div><strong>coéquipiers</strong> This is a French noun meaning "teammates" or "team members". It is in the plural form here, to match with "quelques". The word agrees in number and gender with what it refers to.</div>','<h2>Je pourrai venir dans quelques heures.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the French first-person singular pronoun, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>pourrai</strong> This is the first-person singular future tense of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It indicates a future capability or possibility.</div><div><strong>venir</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French, meaning "to come". It’s used here with "pourrai" to express what the subject is able to do in the future.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition that can be translated as "in" in this context. It indicates that the action will take place after a certain period.</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This means "a few" and is used here to specify an approximate amount of time.</div><div><strong>heures</strong> This is the French word for "hours". It’s the unit of time being referred to in the sentence.</div>','<h2>Vous me connaîtrez mieux dans quelques jours.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used here as the subject of the sentence and can also denote a formal address or refer to more than one person.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is a first-person singular object pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It indicates that the action of the verb is being done to the speaker.</div><div><strong>connaîtrez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural future tense form of the verb "connaître", which means "to know" or "to be acquainted with". The future tense suggests an action that will take place.</div><div><strong>mieux</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "better". In this context, it suggests a comparative level of knowledge or familiarity that will be improved over time.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French that translates to "in" when referring to a period of time in English.</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This means "some" or "a few" in French, indicating an indefinite, but small, quantity.</div><div><strong>jours</strong> This is the plural form of "jour", which means "day" in English. "Quelques jours" translates to "a few days".</div>','<h2>Oui. Je suis en visite et j’ai quelques questions...</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It’s used to affirmatively answer a question or confirm a statement.</div><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state or action of the subject.</div><div><strong>en visite</strong> This phrase translates to "on a visit" or "visiting" in English. It describes the activity or state of the subject, implying that the individual is visiting a place.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>j’ai</strong> This is a contraction of "je" (I) and "ai" (have) — the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It’s used here to indicate possession or in this case to express "I have".</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This word translates to "some" or "a few" in English. It’s an adjective used to describe an unspecified quantity of the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>questions</strong> This is the French word for "questions". It’s the plural form of "question" and is the object of the verb "avoir" in this sentence, referring to what the speaker has.</div>','<h2>Nous serons meilleurs à patiner dans quelques heures.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>serons</strong> This is the first-person plural future tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the future state of being of the subject.</div><div><strong>meilleurs</strong> This is the masculine plural form of the adjective "meilleur", which means "better". It is used to describe the subject "nous", indicating an improvement or superior ability in comparison to the current state.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition which can mean "to", "at", or in other contexts could denote a relationship or a manner.</div><div><strong>patiner</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "patiner", which means "to skate". This verb represents the activity that the subjects will be better at.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This preposition means "in" and is used to indicate a time frame here.</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This word translates to "few" or "some" in English, indicating a small quantity or number.</div><div><strong>heures</strong> This is the noun "heures", which means "hours". It denotes the period of time within which the subjects will become better at skating.</div>','<h2>Vous rencontrerez des gens très importants dans quelques heures.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used here as the subject of the sentence and can also denote a formal address or refer to more than one person.</div><div><strong>rencontrerez</strong> This is the future tense form of the verb "rencontrer", which means "to meet". It indicates that you will meet someone in the future.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is an indefinite article used in French before plural nouns, equivalent to "some" or "any" in English. It precedes the plural noun "gens".</div><div><strong>gens</strong> This is a plural noun meaning "people" or "persons" in English.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very" in English. It is used to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>importants</strong> This is the plural masculine form of the adjective "important", which means "important" in English. It agrees in gender and number with the noun "gens" that it describes.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition meaning "in" in English. It is used here to indicate a time frame.</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This word translates to "a few" in English. It modifies the noun "heures", giving a sense of the amount of time.</div><div><strong>heures</strong> This is the plural form of the noun "heure", which means "hour" in English. It indicates the time frame in which the meeting with important people will occur.</div>','<h2>J’ai la sensation qu’ils jugent les autres.</h2><div><strong>J’ai</strong> This is the first-person singular of the present tense of the verb "avoir", which means "to have" in English. In this context, it indicates possession of a feeling or sensation.</div><div><strong>la sensation</strong> This phrase means "the feeling" or "the sensation" in English. It’s a feminine noun followed by an article that denotes the feeling experienced by the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>qu’ils</strong> This is a contraction of "que" (that) and "ils" (they). "Que" is a conjunction introducing a dependent clause, while "ils" is the third-person plural subject pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English.</div><div><strong>jugent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "juger", which means "to judge". It describes the action being performed by "ils" (they).</div><div><strong>les autres</strong> This phrase translates to "the others" in English. "Les" is the definite article for plural nouns and "autres" is the plural form of "autre", meaning "other". It collectively refers to other people who are being judged by "ils" (they).</div>','<h2>Un parapluie est différent des autres.</h2><div><strong>Un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It’s used here to introduce the noun "parapluie".</div><div><strong>parapluie</strong> This is a French word meaning "umbrella". It is a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It connects the subject "parapluie" to its descriptor.</div><div><strong>différent</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "different". Here, it’s used to describe how the umbrella is not the same as the others, it agrees with the masculine singular noun "parapluie".</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "de" and the definite article "les", meaning "of the" or "from the" in English. It is used here to indicate a relationship of difference from a group of other items.</div><div><strong>autres</strong> This word is the plural form of "autre", a French adjective meaning "other". As a plural noun, it here refers to "others", implying other umbrellas or other things in context.</div>','<h2>Elle doit répondre à son autre appel aussi.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>doit</strong> This is a conjugation of the French verb "devoir" in the third-person singular. "Devoir" means "to have to" or "must", so "doit" translates to "must" or "has to".</div><div><strong>répondre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the French verb meaning "to answer" or "to reply". In this sentence, it is used after "doit", as "devoir" is followed by an infinitive when indicating an obligation.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is the French preposition for "to". In this context, it is used before "son autre appel" to express what she needs to answer to.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective, which means "his", "her", or "its" in English. In this sentence, it denotes possession, implying that the call belongs to the subject ("she").</div><div><strong>autre</strong> This is an adjective in French that means "other". It is used to specify which call she needs to answer in addition to a previous one or other activity.</div><div><strong>appel</strong> This is the French word for "call". Here it is used in the context of a telephone call or similar communication.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This word translates to "also" or "as well" in English. It indicates that answering the call is an additional action to something else.</div>','<h2>Elle n’entend rien d’autre que sa musique.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>n’</strong> This is a contraction of "ne", which is a negation particle. It is used before vowels or mute h. In writing, "ne" or its contracted form is used with another negative word to negate a verb. </div><div><strong>entend</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "entendre", which means "to hear". Here it indicates what the subject does or does not do.</div><div><strong>rien</strong> This is a French indefinite pronoun meaning "nothing". It’s used here in conjunction with "ne" to negate the verb "entend", indicating the subject hears nothing.</div><div><strong>d’autre</strong> This expression translates to "else" or "other" in English. It modifies "rien" to emphasize that she hears nothing else.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a French conjunction or relative pronoun that means "than" or "that" in English. Here it is used to compare and show the only thing she hears is her music.</div><div><strong>sa</strong> This is the possessive adjective in the third-person singular feminine form, meaning "her". It’s used here to show ownership or relation to the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>musique</strong> This is a French noun meaning "music". It is the object of the sentence, indicating what the subject hears exclusively.</div>','<h2>Ayons le courage de s’aider les uns les autres.</h2><div><strong>Ayons</strong> This is the first-person plural imperative form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this context, "ayons" is an exhortation or command to "let’s have" or "we should have".</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article for masculine singular nouns in French, translating to "the" in English. It’s used here to specifically refer to the noun "courage".</div><div><strong>courage</strong> This noun means "courage" or "bravery" in English. It is a masculine noun in French.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition is equivalent to "to" or "of" in English. It is used here to introduce the infinitive that expresses a specific action.</div><div><strong>s’aider</strong> This is the reflexive form of the verb "aider", which means "to help". In the reflexive form "s’aider", it means "to help each other" or "to help oneself".</div><div><strong>les uns les autres</strong> This is a French expression meaning "each other" or "one another". "Les uns" translates to "the ones" and "les autres" translates to "the others".</div>','<h2>Elle jouerait avec les autres enfants, mais elle est très timide.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>jouerait</strong> This is the third-person singular conditional tense form of the verb "jouer", which means "to play". The conditional mood expresses a hypothetical situation or action.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "with". It indicates association or companionship.</div><div><strong>les autres</strong> This phrase means "the others". "Les" is the definite article for plural nouns and "autres" is the plural form of "autre" meaning "other".</div><div><strong>enfants</strong> This is the French word for "children". It’s a noun in plural form.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect contrasting clauses.</div><div><strong>elle est</strong> "Elle" again meaning "she" and "est" is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", meaning "to be". The phrase "elle est" is stating something about her being or condition.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is the French word for "very". It’s an adverb used to intensify an adjective.</div><div><strong>timide</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "shy" or "timid". Here, it describes the personality trait of the subject, "elle". This adjective is in the feminine singular form to agree with the feminine subject "elle".</div>','<h2>Vous partageriez votre nourriture avec les autres, mais vous n’en avez pas assez.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used here to address the reader or listener directly and can also denote a formal address or refer to more than one person.</div><div><strong>partageriez</strong> This is the second-person conditional form of the verb "partager", which means "to share" in English. The conditional mood is used to express what you would do under certain circumstances.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "your" in English. It indicates that the noun it modifies belongs to the person or people being addressed.</div><div><strong>nourriture</strong> This is a French noun meaning "food" or "nourishment". It’s the object of the verb "partageriez" in this sentence.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This French preposition means "with" in English. It indicates accompaniment or association.</div><div><strong>les autres</strong> This phrase combines the definite article "les", which is "the" in English, and "autres", meaning "others". It refers to a group of people besides the one being addressed or involved.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This is the French word for "but". It’s a conjunction used to introduce a contrast or exception to what has been said previously.</div><div><strong>vous</strong> Repeated for emphasis in this context, it is the second-person pronoun "you".</div><div><strong>n’</strong> This is the contraction of "ne", which is a negation particle used in French to negate a verb.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This is a pronoun that can mean "of it", "of them", "some", or "any" in English, often used to replace a phrase introduced by "de". In this context, it refers back to "nourriture".</div><div><strong>avez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have" in English. Despite "vous" being used for both singular and plural forms, "avez" here indicates that the sentence could be addressed to a single person or multiple people.</div><div><strong>pas assez</strong> This phrase means "not enough" in English. "Pas" is the negation word that comes after "ne" to form the negation, and "assez" means "enough".</div>','<h2>Elle a emporté n’importe quoi.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this case, it’s used as an auxiliary verb to form the past tense, known as the passé composé.</div><div><strong>emporté</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "emporter", which means "to take" or "to carry away". Combined with "a", it completes the passé composé, indicating a completed action in the past.</div><div><strong>n’importe quoi</strong> This is a French expression that translates to "anything" or "whatever" in English. It usually implies something nonsensical, random, or of little value.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);